Basic math operations in plain English.

Use core.

Publicly, to find the absolute value of x (a whole number), named abs_i64, giving a whole number.
Give back 0 minus x if x is less than 0, otherwise give back x.

Publicly, to find the absolute value of x (a decimal), named abs_f64, giving a decimal.
Give back 0.0 if x equals 0.0.
Give back 0.0 minus x if x is less than 0.0, otherwise give back x.

Publicly, to find the smaller of a and b (whole numbers), named min_i64, giving a whole number.
Give back a if a is less than b, otherwise give back b.

Publicly, to find the larger of a and b (whole numbers), named max_i64, giving a whole number.
Give back a if a is more than b, otherwise give back b.

Publicly, to find the smaller of a and b (decimals), named min_f64, giving a decimal.
Give back a if a is less than b, otherwise give back b.

Publicly, to find the larger of a and b (decimals), named max_f64, giving a decimal.
Give back a if a is more than b, otherwise give back b.

Publicly, to find the square root of x (a decimal), named sqrt, giving a decimal.
Stop with error "sqrt of negative number" if x is less than 0.0.
Give back 0.0 if x equals 0.0.
Let guess be 1.0.
Set guess to 0.5 times x if x is at least 1.0.
Let iteration be 0.
Until iteration is at least 24, set guess to 0.5 times (guess plus x divided by guess), add 1 to iteration.
Give back guess.

Publicly, to raise base (a whole number) to exponent (a whole number), named pow_i64, giving a whole number.
Give back 0 if exponent is less than 0.
Let result be 1.
Let factor be base.
Let power be exponent.
While power is more than 0, set result to result times factor if power modulo 2 equals 1, set factor to factor times factor, set power to power divided by 2.
Give back result.

Publicly, to clamp value (a whole number) between low (a whole number) and high (a whole number), named clamp_i64, giving a whole number.
Give back low if value is less than low.
Give back high if value is more than high.
Give back value.
